{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1787116018,"nonce":"75+X+bZcfyZ2A/sEQSNqOX7+K2E=","message":"⛔ **OWNER RULING, 2026-08-19: ONE CASE PER CONVERSATION, ALWAYS. Done-when 2 is VOID as written, and stage 2 is built on the premise it reversed.** Read this before continuing the ticket.\n\n**What was ruled**\n- A conversation never holds more than one Case. A new case always means a new thread.\n- `unacked_reminder` is DROPPED as a feature.\n- `channel_threads.state = 'frozen'` is DROPPED.\n- A conversation is not immortal: after a configurable threshold, the next fact BROADCASTS or starts a NEW message rather than appending. That heuristic is to be built and made operator-configurable.\n\n**What this voids**\nDone-when 2 reads: *\"A test asserts two alerts differing on `alertname` and sharing `node` land in ONE conversation under a `group_by: [node]` policy — the case that is impossible today.\"* Two alerts differing on `alertname` are two Alerts and therefore two Cases. Under the ruling they may NOT share a conversation. The clause is not merely unmet, it is now forbidden.\n\n**What replaces the deleted table**\n`alert_groups` is still deleted — that is unchanged. But the conversation becomes **the Case**, not a policy collapse. `alert_cases` already exists, is already the product's first destination (`2c26520` made `/` redirect to `/cases`), and already has exactly the one-per-conversation cardinality the ruling requires. So `conversation_kind` becomes `case | digest` rather than `alert_group | digest`, and no new identity needs inventing.\n\n**What this costs, stated plainly**\n`bf6f8f6` (stage 2) added `notification_policies.group_by` and `Policy.CollapseKey`, whose only purpose was to collapse several cases into one conversation. Under this ruling they decide nothing. **Leaving them would be dead config** — the exact defect four tickets closed tonight (`0457f1f` a dead batch read, `35d4248` `DigestConfig.Limit` that nothing wrote, `39e48e2` queue widths nothing used, `27a1860` three inert settings keys). I am not adding a fifth.\n\nStage 1 (`d76ee0d`, the matcher seeing the alert's full labels) SURVIVES and is worth keeping regardless: policies matching on `node` is useful whether or not `node` groups anything, and it was a real limitation.\n\n**Revised stages**\n| Stage | Was | Now |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | matcher sees full labels | LANDED, unaffected |\n| 2 | `group_by` + collapse key | REVERT — no purpose under the ruling |\n| 3 | `(conversation_kind, conversation_id)` | keep, kinds become `case \\| digest` |\n| 4 | drop `alert_groups` + `group_id` | unchanged; conversation re-points at the Case |\n| 5 | remove the nine `/alert-groups*` paths | unchanged |\n| 6 | subject-kind binding + count-over-window | keep; ADD the rollover threshold, which is now what ends a conversation |\n| 7 | SPEC/ADR/CONTEXT | unchanged, plus recording this ruling |\n\n**Two features drop out entirely**, each getting its own ticket rather than riding along here: `unacked_reminder` (the once-per-generation latch at `reminders.go:119` goes with it, which also removes the silent-nag hazard its own comment warns about) and `frozen` (which is `e5c060b`, now decided — delete rather than wire).\n\n⚠️ The rollover heuristic is NEW SCOPE this ticket did not have. It is the answer to \"what ends a conversation\", which the ticket never addressed: it proposed `channel_threads.generation` justified by channel archival and never said what ADVANCES it.","files":null}]}